WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.130
Let's start to formalize the problem of learning a good word embedding.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.990
When you implement an algorithm to learn a word embedding, what you end up learning

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.190
is an embedding matrix.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.710
Let's take a look at what that means.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.310
Let's say as usual we're using our 10,000 word vocabulary.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:28.670
So the vocabulary is A, Aaron, Orange, Zulu, maybe also unknown word is a token.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:37.150
What we're going to do is learn an embedding matrix E, which is going to be a 300 dimensional

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:42.190
by 10,000 dimensional matrix.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:48.350
If you have 10,000 words in vocabulary or maybe 10,001 if the unknown word token is one extra

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.910
token.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:54.590
And the columns of this matrix will be the different embeddings for the 10,000 different

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.550
words you have in your vocabulary.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:03.990
So orange was word number 6257 in our vocabulary of 10,000 words.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:14.090
So one piece of notation we'll use is that 0 6257 was the one hot vector with zeros everywhere

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.430
and a one in position 6257.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:25.950
And so this would be a 10,000 dimensional vector with a one in just one position.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.430
So this isn't quite drawn to scale.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:33.190
Yes, this should be as tall as the embedding matrix on the left is wide.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:40.770
And if the embedding matrix is called capital E, then notice that if you take E and multiply

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:52.310
it by this one hot vector by 0 of 6257, then this will be a 300 dimensional vector.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:02:00.430
So E is 300 by 10,000 and O is 10,000 by 1.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:06.910
So the product will be 300 by 1, so it will be a 300 dimensional vector.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:13.810
And notice that to compute the first element of this vector, of this 300 dimensional vector,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:20.150
what you do is you would multiply the first row of the matrix E with this.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.350
But all of these elements are 0 except for element 6257.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:28.730
And so you end up with 0 times this, 0 times this, 0 times this, and so on.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:35.490
And then 1 times whatever this is, and 0 times this, 0 times this, and so on.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:43.110
And so you end up with the first element as whatever is that element up there under

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.630
the orange column.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.630
And then for the second element of this 300 dimensional vector we're computing, you

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:55.430
would take the vector 0 6257 and multiply it by the second row of the matrix E.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:02.890
So again you have 0 times this plus 0 times this plus 0 times all of these other elements

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:09.030
and then 1 times this and then 0 times everything else and add that together, so you end up

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:18.240
with this and so on as you go down the rest of this column.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.200
So that's why the embedding matrix E times this one hot vector here winds up selecting

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:30.880
out this 300 dimensional column corresponding to the word orange.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:39.320
So this is going to be equal to E 6257 which is the notation we're going to use to represent

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.240
the embedding vector, the 300 by 1 dimensional vector for the word orange.
